/T (A collision occurs when two keys get mapped to the same index. There are several ways of handling collisions.)
/E (A collision occurs when two keys get mapped to the same index. There are several ways of handling collisions.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
518 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 309 0 R
/T (Linear probing If a pair is hashed to a slot which is already occupied, it searches linearly for the next free slot in the table. Chaining The hash table will be an array of linked lists. All keys mapping to the same index will be stored as linked list nodes at that index. Resizing the hash table The size of the hash table can be increased in order to spread the hash entries further apart. A threshold value signifies the  percentage of the hash-table that needs to be occupied before resizing. A hash table with a threshold of 0.6 would resize when 60%  of the space is occupied. As a convention, the size of the hash-table is doubled. This can be memory intensive.)
/E (Linear probing If a pair is hashed to a slot which is already occupied, it searches linearly for the next free slot in the table. Chaining The hash table will be an array of linked lists. All keys mapping to the same index will be stored as linked list nodes at that index. Resizing the hash table The size of the hash table can be increased in order to spread the hash entries further apart. A threshold value signifies the  percentage of the hash-table that needs to be occupied before resizing. A hash table with a threshold of 0.6 would resize when 60%  of the space is occupied. As a convention, the size of the hash-table is doubled. This can be memory intensive.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
519 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 309 0 R
/T (Hash tables have incredibly quick lookups, but remember that we need a reliable collision solution; normally, we don't need to worry about this because our language in the computer beneath the hood takes care of it for us. It allows us to respond quickly, and depending on the type of hash table, such as maps in JavaScript, we can have flexible keys instead of an array with 0 1 2 3 only numbered indexes. The disadvantage of hash tables is that they are unordered. It's difficult to go through everything in an orderly fashion. Furthermore, it has slow key iteration. That is, if I want to retrieve all the keys from a hash table, I have to navigate the  entire memory space.)
/E (Hash tables have incredibly quick lookups, but remember that we need a reliable collision solution; normally, we don't need to worry about this because our language in the computer beneath the hood takes care of it for us. It allows us to respond quickly, and depending on the type of hash table, such as maps in JavaScript, we can have flexible keys instead of an array with 0 1 2 3 only numbered indexes. The disadvantage of hash tables is that they are unordered. It's difficult to go through everything in an orderly fashion. Furthermore, it has slow key iteration. That is, if I want to retrieve all the keys from a hash table, I have to navigate the  entire memory space.)
/Pg 13 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
