/T (Singly linked list The singly linked list \(SLL\) is a linear data structure comprising of nodes chained together in a single direction. Each node contains  a data member holding useful information, and a pointer to the next node. The problem with this structure is that it only allows us to  traverse forward, i.e., we cannot iterate back to a previous node if required. This is where the doubly linked list \(DLL\) shines. DLLs  are an extension of basic linked lists with only one difference.)
/E (Singly linked list The singly linked list \(SLL\) is a linear data structure comprising of nodes chained together in a single direction. Each node contains  a data member holding useful information, and a pointer to the next node. The problem with this structure is that it only allows us to  traverse forward, i.e., we cannot iterate back to a previous node if required. This is where the doubly linked list \(DLL\) shines. DLLs  are an extension of basic linked lists with only one difference.)
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [9]
>>
