/T (Quick Sort has a time complexity of O\(n log n\) on average, making it one of the most efficient sorting algorithms for large datasets. However, in the worst case \(e.g., when the pivot element always selects the maximum or minimum element of the array\), Quick Sort can have a time complexity of O\(n^2\). This worst-case scenario can be avoided by selecting a good pivot element, such as the median or a random element. Additionally, Quick Sort is an in-place sorting algorithm, meaning that it does not require additional memory beyond the original array.)
/E (Quick Sort has a time complexity of O\(n log n\) on average, making it one of the most efficient sorting algorithms for large datasets. However, in the worst case \(e.g., when the pivot element always selects the maximum or minimum element of the array\), Quick Sort can have a time complexity of O\(n^2\). This worst-case scenario can be avoided by selecting a good pivot element, such as the median or a random element. Additionally, Quick Sort is an in-place sorting algorithm, meaning that it does not require additional memory beyond the original array.)
