/T (A sparse graph is a graph that has relatively few edges compared to the number of vertices it has. In other words, in a sparse graph, most pairs of vertices are not directly connected by an edge. Sparse graphs are commonly found in real-world applications, such as social networks or transportation networks, where the number of connections or relationships between entities is much smaller than the total number of entities. Because of their sparsity, some algorithms can be faster to execute on sparse graphs since they don't have to check as many edges.)
